window.LLD_CARDS = [{"n":0.1,"ti":"Classes, Objects & References","f":"p1-classes-objects.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"After Session a = new Session(\"u1\"); Session b = a; b.extend(30); what does a.ttlMinutes() return? (A new session starts at 30.)","o":["30, because only b was extended","60","It doesn't compile","It depends on when the garbage collector runs"],"a":1,"w":"b = a copies the reference, so a and b point at the same object. Extending it through b changes the one object both variables see, so it is now 60."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Classes, Objects & References","f":"p1-classes-objects.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"static void swap(Session x, Session y) { Session t = x; x = y; y = t; } is called as swap(a, b) , where a is user u1's session and b is u2's. What does a.userId() print afterwards?","o":["u2, because the sessions were swapped","It throws NullPointerException","u1, because only the method's copies of the references were swapped","u1, because swap made copies of both Session objects"],"a":2,"w":"Java copies each reference into x and y . The method swaps those two local copies, then its frame is discarded. The caller's a and b never changed. No object was copied either: only references are passed."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Classes, Objects & References","f":"p1-classes-objects.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Inside a method, Session s = new Session(\"u1\"); runs. Where do the variable s and the Session object live?","o":["s (a reference) is in the method's stack frame; the object is on the heap","Both are on the stack, because s is a local variable","Both are on the heap","s is on the heap; the object is in the stack frame"],"a":0,"w":"Local variables, including references, live in the frame of the call that declares them. Every object created with new lives on the heap. That is why the object can outlive the method, for example if it is returned or stored in a field."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Classes, Objects & References","f":"p1-classes-objects.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"When does a Session object become eligible for garbage collection?","o":["As soon as the method that created it returns","When its reference count drops to zero, so two objects pointing at each other are never collected","When you call System.gc()","When no chain of references from a GC root (a live frame, a static field) reaches it any more"],"a":3,"w":"Java's collectors use reachability from GC roots. An object returned or stored in a field survives its creating method. Cycles are collected once nothing outside reaches them. System.gc() is only a hint and never frees a reachable object."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Classes, Objects & References","f":"p1-classes-objects.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why is private static int activeSessions , incremented and decremented from constructors and a close() method, a risky design?","o":["Static fields are stored on the stack, so the value is lost between calls","It is one global value shared by every test and every thread: tests leak state into each other, and concurrent updates can be lost","Static fields can't be changed after the class loads","Each Session object gets its own copy, so the count is always 1"],"a":1,"w":"A static field has exactly one copy for the whole program. That makes it global mutable state: hidden from constructors, shared across tests, and racy under concurrency unless guarded. A counter object passed to whoever needs it avoids all three problems."}];
